    {
      "text": "%spark\nretailDF\n.select(f.col(\"description\"), f.regexp_replace(f.col(\"description\"), regexString, \"Sujeet\").as(\"regexReplace\"),\n        f.translate(f.col(\"description\"),\"LEET\",\"MFFU\").alias(\"translate\"),\n        f.regexp_extract(f.col(\"description\"),regexExtractString,1 ).as(\"regexExtract\") ) //where(f.col(\"regexExtract\") === \"\")-> if no value extracted will return empty \n.show(5,false)",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/scala",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "scala",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "8596aff0aa7e4afdbfe1c"
    },
    {
      "text": "####Regular Expression\n`*There are two key functions in Spark that you’ll need in\norder to perform regular expression tasks: regexp_extract and regexp_replace.\n\n1. regexp_replace(col, regextString, replacement)`\n2. regexp_extract(col, regextString, index)\n3. translate(col, \"oldValue:String\", \"newValue:String\"):  replace fiven characters with other charecters\n\n*//Sometimes, rather than extracting values, we simply want to check for their existence*\n\n1. col(\"description\").contains(\"some value\")\n\n    `we can do same in the sql  using:  instr(description, 'BLACK') >= 1`\n\n2.",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/markdown",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "markdown",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "11083a27237143ec8444a"
    },
    {
      "text": "%spark\ncustomerDF.where(f.col(\"gender\").equalTo(\"Female\")).select(\"name\",\"cust_id\",\"city\",\"age\").show(5,false)   //Scala uses  ===  for equality, =!=  for not equal , in case we don't wnat to use equalTo",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/scala",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "scala",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "3b17d1427c7c4e38acfb4"
    },
    {
      "text": "%spark\nimport org.apache.spark.sql.{functions => f,types, DataFrameReader, DataFrameNaFunctions => na, DataFrameStatFunctions => sf}",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/scala",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "scala",
